      "url": "https://web.mit.edu/",
      "title": "MIT - Massachusetts Institute of Technology",
      "text": "Skip to content ↓\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology\nEducation\nResearch\nInnovation\nAdmissions + Aid\nCampus Life\nNews\nAlumni\nAbout MIT\nLifelong Learning\nGive\nSearch\nExplore websites, people, and locations\nWhat are you looking for?\nTop resources for\nprospective students\ncurrent students\nfaculty & staff\nalumni\nparents & families\nall resources\nMIT's response to government activity\nRead the latest about government activity affecting MIT, as well as information on MIT’s contributions to the nation.\n\nThe LLM Election Observatory shows how AI chatbots are responding to election questions. “People may not realize that what their favorite chatbot tells them about a political issue may be quite different from what their friend’s favorite chatbot tells them,” Chara Podimata says.\n\nNov 3, 2026 Full story Share: \nTwitter\n \nFacebook\n Explore more spotlights\nResearch and Education that Matter\nPersonalized cancer vaccines\n\nEngineers have developed a nanoscale particle for delivering personalized cancer vaccines. The particle could be adapted to carry many different antigens, to potentially prevent or treat individual patients’ tumors.\n\nBetter intestinal treatments\n\nEngineers designed a robotic enteroscope that can move easily through the narrow passage of the small intestine. They hope to deploy it to perform imaging, deliver drugs, and remove small polyps or tissue samples for biopsy.\n\nNew approach to nuclear\n\nFounded by Jake Jurewicz ’14, SM ’15, Blue Energy manufactures giant pieces of nuclear reactors at shipyards and ships them on barges for final assembly. The startup aims to rejuvenate the nuclear power industry with a new manufacturing approach.\n\nBioadhesive technology\n\nAn MIT spinout is making surgical stitching more like applying tape: SanaHeal has developed a biologically compatible adhesive that sticks to wet tissues. Its product was inspired by sticky materials in nature, like barnacles and spider webs.\n\nSupporting our Jewish community\n\nThis fact sheet offers a high-level summary of the many steps MIT leaders have taken to support our vibrant Jewish community.\n\nBenefits for all Americans\n\nMIT’s vital research and education — on topics such as human health, energy innovation, quantum computing, and advanced manufacturing — continue to advance national security, economic competitiveness, and quality of life for all Americans.\n\nWant more about MIT?\n\nExplore more spotlights, or subscribe to receive daily or weekly doses of MIT in your inbox.\n\nSubscribe\n\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology\n\n77 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, MA, USA\nVisit\nMap\nEvents\nPeople\nJobs\nContact\nPrivacy\nAccessibility\nSocial Media Hub \nMIT@twitter\n \nMIT@facebook\n \nMIT@youtube\n \nMIT@instagram",
